;;; A query's projection classes merged the int `1` and the name `"1"` —
;;; regression `classes-int-name`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One candidate relation, `pick`, over the objects `A` and `B`. Rule `a`
;;; concludes `(got 1)` from `(pick A)`, rule `b` `(got "1")`, a string as
;;; the argument, from `(pick B)`. The query asks `(got ?v)` over all of R.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A string is natural-language text, never a value (Q-M1e2.23), so the
;;; program is refused at the parse, at rule `b`'s `"1"`, on every command
;;; (exit 1, `ein test`: 2). A run of digits is a name (Q-M1e2.24), so the
;;; case this file was written for can no longer be written.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `86227f9a` the string made `1` a name of the program, apart from the
;;; int `1` rule `a` concludes over, and the classes were keyed by the goal's
;;; answers as printed text, in which the int `1` and the name `"1"` were one
;;; text, `(got 1)`: `ein solve FILE` said *8 states → 2 projection classes*,
;;; every state holding either rule's conclusion in one class, where the four
;;; classes were none, `(got 1)` alone, `(got "1")` alone, and both; in a
;;; release build and a debug one.
;;;
;;;

(relation pick Node)
(is-a A Node) (is-a B Node)
(rule a () :match (pick A) :assert (got 1))
(rule b () :match (pick B) :assert (got "1"))
(query :goal (got ?v))
